The most attractive short-feedback asymmetric setup right now is actually XOM, not a speculative moonshot. The market has supplied an observable causal chain:

Iran risk → oil >$91 → inflation concern/yields ↑ → growth stocks ↓ while energy cash-flow expectations ↑ → XOM relative strength. 

That’s a much cleaner Moneyball signal than finding a tiny ticker capable of doubling.

Deep Meta — the first interesting pattern

The Atlas just discovered a potentially important second-order interaction.

AI investment itself is contributing to enormous borrowing requirements. Reuters reports that Alphabet, Amazon and Meta alone have borrowed nearly $220 billion in 2026, contributing to competition for capital and higher real yields. Higher yields then reduce the relative attractiveness of long-duration AI equities. 

So part of the AI boom potentially contains its own negative feedback loop:

AI\ demand
\rightarrow
datacenter\ capex
\rightarrow
borrowing
\rightarrow
capital\ scarcity
\rightarrow
yields\uparrow
\rightarrow
AI\ valuation\ multiples\downarrow

That’s Ouroboros Radar material.

Even more interestingly, CAT occupies both sides. Caterpillar has benefited from enormous AI-driven demand for power-generation and construction equipment, with record backlog, while today’s rising-yield/industrial environment punished the stock. 

So our old representation—

CAT=\text{industrial}

—is inferior to:

CAT=
\text{physical infrastructure derivative of AI}
+
\text{cyclical industrial exposure}.

That is a genuine Atlas improvement.

Meanwhile NVDA has acquired another unusual feedback edge. NVIDIA agreed this week to guarantee up to $105 billion supporting an OpenAI Ohio data-center lease, meaning it is increasingly participating in financing the infrastructure that ultimately purchases its own chips. Reuters reports the structure may involve both debt and equity. 

That does not make the economics circular by definition. But it creates a variable Goblin should explicitly monitor:

\text{AI supplier}
\rightarrow
\text{AI infrastructure financing}
\rightarrow
\text{customer capacity}
\rightarrow
\text{AI supplier demand}.

The interesting question is no longer simply “How many GPUs will customers buy?” It is increasingly “How much financing must the ecosystem absorb to sustain the demand trajectory?”

That connects NVDA, CAT, Treasury yields, hyperscaler debt and eventually even JPM into one causal graph rather than six unrelated stock analyses.

Calibration ledger

This is only the first resolved pulse, so assigning a statistically meaningful Brier score would be fake precision. We didn’t preregister sufficiently exact binary probabilities yesterday for every thesis. I’m therefore preserving the first run as qualitative calibration only rather than retroactively inventing numbers.

Current scoreboard:

Strong confirmation: XOM
Confirmation: LLY
Correct restraint: NVDA
Unresolved catalyst: WMT
Useful failed signal: CAT
Control/no forced inference: JPM

And CAT gave us the most valuable lesson of the run:

\boxed{\text{relative strength without participation is evidence, not confirmation}}

The Goblin now needs repeated relative strength plus participation/causal confirmation before upgrading that pattern.

So the first Realitsea wake already contains something useful: the system made money on paper conceptually, but more importantly, it found one of its own bad planks and marked it for replacement rather than explaining the failure away.